 ## Overview
-Solid Voxelization fills the interior of a mesh with voxels, producing a fully occupied volume rather than just a surface shell. It builds on [Ray-Cast Style Voxel Filling](1502-ray-cast-style-voxel-filling.md) by using a two-level grid model to determine which voxels are interior. It differs from [Slice Filling (Simple)](1691-slice-filling-simple.md) by explicitly handling the connectivity of the volume via flood-fill or axis-aligned projection strategies to ensure watertightness, even if the input mesh is not strictly watertight [S2, unofficial].
+Solid Voxelization fills the interior of a mesh with voxels (solid/bulk) rather than just the surface, using a two-level grid model [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Ray-Cast Style Voxel Filling](1502-ray-cast-style-voxel-filling.md) by determining interior voxels via a two-pass approach with bitsets and a final inversion step [S2, unofficial]. It differs from [Slice Filling (Simple)](1691-slice-filling-simple.md) by explicitly handling volume connectivity via flood-fill or axis-aligned projection strategies to ensure watertightness, even if the input mesh is not strictly watertight [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A triangle mesh (vertices and indices), a voxel size (`gridVoxelSize`), and a bounding box for the output grid.
-**Outputs:** A 3D grid (bitmask) where set bits indicate solid voxels.
